# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'Haggai'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'prophets'
SUBTITLE = 'Consider Your Ways, Build the House, and the Glory of the Latter House'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Haggai records four words of the LORD “by Haggai the prophet unto Zerubbabel the son of Shealtiel, governor of Judah, and to Joshua the son of Josedech, the high priest” (Hag. 1:1). The people are told to consider their ways and build the house; the LORD says He is with them; He promises to shake the heavens and the earth and fill the house with glory; and Zerubbabel is chosen as a signet (Hag. 1:5, 8; 2:4, 7, 9, 23).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'This book belongs entirely to the Prophecy Program, where the LORD speaks to Judah’s governor, Joshua the high priest and the people about the house, His covenant from Egypt, and the shaking of the nations (Hag. 1:1; 2:5–7, 22–23).')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Haggai', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — Consider Your Ways (Ch. 1)'), ('p', 'The people are told to go up to the mountain, bring wood and build the house (Hag. 1:1, 5, 7–8).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — The Glory of the Latter House (Ch. 2:1–9)'), ('p', 'The LORD says, “be strong… for I am with you,” and promises to fill the house with glory (Hag. 2:4–7, 9).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — From This Day I Will Bless (Ch. 2:10–19)'), ('p', 'The people are told to consider, and the LORD says, “from this day will I bless you” (Hag. 2:19).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — Zerubbabel the Signet (Ch. 2:20–23)'), ('p', 'The LORD will shake the heavens and the earth and take Zerubbabel as a signet (Hag. 2:21–23).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Haggai', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>Consider Your Ways</b> — “Thus saith the LORD of hosts; Consider your ways” (Hag. 1:5).', '<b>Build the House</b> — “Go up to the mountain, and bring wood, and build the house; and I will take pleasure in it” (Hag. 1:8).', '<b>I Am With You</b> — “Be strong… and work: for I am with you, saith the LORD of hosts” (Hag. 2:4).', '<b>The Covenant From Egypt</b> — “According to the word that I covenanted with you when ye came out of Egypt, so my spirit remaineth among you: fear ye not” (Hag. 2:5).', '<b>The Shaking</b> — “I will shake all nations, and the desire of all nations shall come: and I will fill this house with glory” (Hag. 2:7).', '<b>Peace in This Place</b> — “In this place will I give peace, saith the LORD of hosts” (Hag. 2:9).', '<b>The Signet</b> — “will I take thee, O Zerubbabel, my servant… and will make thee as a signet: for I have chosen thee” (Hag. 2:23).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Haggai', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how Haggai points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Desire of All Nations</b> — The words “the desire of all nations shall come” (Hag. 2:7) are reported as written, with no further identification made here.', '<b>The Latter House</b> — “The glory of this latter house shall be greater than of the former” (Hag. 2:9).', '<b>The Signet</b> — Zerubbabel, the LORD’s servant, is made “as a signet” (Hag. 2:23); the line of Judah’s governor is recorded as written.'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in Haggai', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Haggai speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. Haggai operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Haggai in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', 'Haggai reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['Zerubbabel the governor of Judah and Joshua the high priest (Hag. 1:1; 2:4)', 'the covenant made when Israel came out of Egypt (Hag. 2:5)', 'the house to be built and filled with glory (Hag. 2:7, 9)', 'the shaking of the kingdoms of the heathen (Hag. 2:22)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Haggai that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Haggai contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['Zerubbabel and Joshua — “came the word of the LORD by Haggai the prophet unto Zerubbabel… and to Joshua the son of Josedech, the high priest.” (Hag. 1:1).', 'Build the House — “build the house; and I will take pleasure in it, and I will be glorified, saith the LORD.” (Hag. 1:8).', 'The Covenant From Egypt — “According to the word that I covenanted with you when ye came out of Egypt.” (Hag. 2:5).', 'The Shaking — “I will shake the heavens, and the earth, and the sea, and the dry land.” (Hag. 2:6).', 'The House Filled — “I will fill this house with glory, saith the LORD of hosts.” (Hag. 2:7).', 'The Latter House — “The glory of this latter house shall be greater than of the former.” (Hag. 2:9).', 'The Kingdoms Overthrown — “I will overthrow the throne of kingdoms, and I will destroy the strength of the kingdoms of the heathen.” (Hag. 2:22).', 'The Signet — “will I take thee, O Zerubbabel, my servant, the son of Shealtiel, saith the LORD, and will make thee as a signet.” (Hag. 2:23).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Haggai Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Haggai and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Haggai teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Haggai', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['Zerubbabel and Joshua', '“came the word of the LORD by Haggai the prophet unto Zerubbabel… and to Joshua the son of Josedech, the high priest.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Hag. 1:1'], ['Build the House', '“build the house; and I will take pleasure in it, and I will be glorified, saith the LORD.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Hag. 1:8'], ['The Covenant From Egypt', '“According to the word that I covenanted with you when ye came out of Egypt.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Hag. 2:5'], ['The Shaking', '“I will shake the heavens, and the earth, and the sea, and the dry land.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Hag. 2:6'], ['The House Filled', '“I will fill this house with glory, saith the LORD of hosts.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Hag. 2:7'], ['The Latter House', '“The glory of this latter house shall be greater than of the former.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Hag. 2:9'], ['The Kingdoms Overthrown', '“I will overthrow the throne of kingdoms, and I will destroy the strength of the kingdoms of the heathen.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Hag. 2:22'], ['The Signet', '“will I take thee, O Zerubbabel, my servant, the son of Shealtiel, saith the LORD, and will make thee as a signet.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Hag. 2:23']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Haggai that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what Haggai teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['Consider Your Ways', '“Thus saith the LORD of hosts; Consider your ways.”', 'Hag. 1:5'], ['Be Strong and Work', '“be strong, all ye people of the land, saith the LORD, and work: for I am with you.”', 'Hag. 2:4'], ['His Blessing', '“from this day will I bless you.”', 'Hag. 2:19']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Haggai without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to Haggai in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Consider your ways</b> — Put the LORD’s work first (Hag. 1:5).', '<b>Be strong and work</b> — The LORD is with you (Hag. 2:4).', '<b>Receive His blessing</b> — From this day He will bless (Hag. 2:19).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Haggai', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Haggai in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['Consider Your Ways (Ch. 1) — Build the house. <i>(Hag. 1:1, 5, 7–8)</i>', 'The Glory of the Latter House (Ch. 2:1–9) — I am with you. <i>(Hag. 2:4–7, 9)</i>', 'From This Day I Will Bless (Ch. 2:10–19) — Consider. <i>(Hag. 2:19)</i>', 'Zerubbabel the Signet (Ch. 2:20–23) — The shaking and the choice. <i>(Hag. 2:21–23)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Haggai, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward putting God first, strength for the work, and trust in His presence without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Haggai simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, Haggai stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]
